# NON-SECRET per-environment host config for the staging host (Lightsail
# nano_3_0, 512MB): container sizing, and the adapter selectors that are a
# constant of this environment rather than an operational choice. The deploy
# reconciles these into /srv/footbag/env on every run, keyed on FOOTBAG_ENV;
# this committed file is the source of truth, so a hand edit on the host does
# not survive the next deploy.
#
# What belongs here and what does not. A value belongs here when it is never an
# operational choice: it changes only when the code changes, so it is reviewed
# with the code. A value an operator could legitimately want the other way
# without a code change is an arming switch, owned by Terraform and synced from
# Parameter Store. An identifier naming a resource Terraform creates is also a
# Terraform-published parameter, not a line here.
#
# Secrets (session, origin-verify, internal-event, API/webhook keys) live in
# /srv/footbag/env (operator-managed) and AWS SSM, NEVER here. Never add a
# secret, API key, identity, hostname, or URL to this file.

# Adapter selectors that are constants of a deployed environment. Identical to
# production apart from the captcha: a live challenge on a host that serves no
# widget is not a preference but a broken environment, because a tester has no
# way to solve one.
MEDIA_STORAGE_ADAPTER=s3
SECRETS_ADAPTER=live
JWT_SIGNER=kms
CAPTCHA_ADAPTER=stub

# Limits are runaway guards, not reservations: their sum (528M) deliberately
# exceeds what the ~419M host can hand out, and the residual is carried by the
# transcode admission floor (an encode, the only large spike, cannot start
# when host memory is low) plus the disk swapfile. nginx and image are sized
# to their measured peaks; a big-photo Sharp decode may still cgroup-OOM the
# image container, which is an expected, contained, auto-restarting condition.
NGINX_MEMORY_LIMIT=48M
WEB_MEMORY_LIMIT=192M
WORKER_MEMORY_LIMIT=96M
IMAGE_MEMORY_LIMIT=192M
IMAGE_MAX_CONCURRENT=1
VIDEO_X264_PRESET=veryfast
VIDEO_X264_THREADS=1
VIDEO_X264_RC_LOOKAHEAD=10
# 720p on the nano host: halves the per-frame encode cost vs 1080p, which is
# the difference between an encode that fits beside the running stack and one
# that starves the host. Production, sized for 1080p, does not set this.
VIDEO_MAX_HEIGHT=720
# Transcode admission floor, in MB of host MemAvailable, below which an encode
# is refused rather than started. Measured on this host at these encoder
# settings rather than assumed, by sampling across a real 720p upload: the host
# sat at 99 MB available, the encode took it to a minimum of 36 MB, and it
# recovered to 136 MB, so the working set is about 60 MB. About 45 MB of that
# went to the disk swapfile at the peak and came back, which is the valve doing
# its job.
#
# 96 = the 60 MB the encode needs, plus the 36 MB that was still free at the
# worst moment of a run this host completed healthily. Refusing below that
# refuses an encode the host has not been shown to absorb. The built-in 128
# default is wrong here for a reason that is about the host rather than the
# encoder: this box settles a few MB under it, so it would refuse every encode
# forever while never being the constraint that mattered.
#
# The floor guards the host; the image container's cgroup ceiling bounds the
# container, and the disk swapfile absorbs the spike. All three are needed:
# this host has 419 MB of RAM against a stack that already uses most of it.
VIDEO_MIN_HOST_AVAILABLE_MB=96
